Group A: Brief Questions (2 Points)
1. Describe information architecture.
Information architecture or IA is the process of correctly ordering and organizing content on a website so that people can quickly locate what they're looking for.
  It primarily addresses navigation, categories, and menus.

2. What is a "Wireframe"?
A wireframe is comparable to a webpage's rough layout or sketch. Before creating the actual design, it displays the locations of elements like the menu, buttons,
  pictures, and text.

3. Describe "Cognitive Friction" in web design.
When a website causes confusion or excessive thought, it is known as cognitive friction. For instance, consumers may struggle and rapidly abandon a website if 
buttons are unclear or the layout is disorganized.

4. Why employ hyphens rather thanrather than using underscores in URL slugs?
Because they make the URL easier to read and are better understood by search engines, hyphens are preferable.
For example: my-product-page is considered good while my_product_page not as much efficient in comparison.

Group B: Extended Questions (4 Points)

5. Compare and contrast Hierarchical (Tree) structure and Linear (Sequential) structure.
A Hierarchical (Tree) structure organizes information in levels, starting from a main page and branching into subpages. It allows users to choose different paths 
and easily explore large amounts of content. This structure is commonly used in websites with many sections.
Example: An e-commerce website with categories and subcategories.
A Linear (Sequential) structure presents information in a fixed order, where users move step by step from one page to the next. It is suitable when information 
must be followed in sequence.
Example: An online tutorial or checkout process.
Hierarchical structures offer flexibility, while linear structures provide guided navigation.

6. Explain the “Three-Click Rule” and its significance in User Experience (UX) design.\
The Three-Click Rule suggests that users should be able to find any information on a website within three clicks from the homepage. The idea behind this rule
is simple: if users have to click too many times, they may get frustrated and leave the website.
In UX design, this rule highlights the importance of clear navigation and well-organized content. It encourages designers to think from the user’s perspective 
and reduce unnecessary complexity. While the rule is not strict or scientific, it acts as a useful guideline to keep websites simple and efficient.
The real significance of the Three-Click Rule lies in improving user satisfaction. When users find what they are looking for quickly, they feel comfortable and
are more likely to stay on the website. In short, fewer clicks mean less confusion, better usability, and a smoother user experience.

7. “Plan before you do.” Explain how Card Sorting and Flowcharts help in planning a website’s structure.
“Plan before you do” is especially important in web design because a poorly planned structure can confuse users, no matter how good the design looks. Tools like
Card Sorting and Flowcharts help designers organize content logically before building the website.
Card Sorting involves writing content topics on cards and asking users or designers to group them in a way that makes sense. This helps identify how users naturally
think about information and what categories feel intuitive to them. As a result, navigation becomes more user-friendly rather than designer-centric.
Flowcharts visually represent how users move from one page to another. They show the sequence of actions, decision points, and page connections. 
Flowcharts help designers spot missing steps, unnecessary pages, or confusing paths before development starts.
Together,thye reduce confusion, save time, and prevent redesigns later. They ensure the website structure is logical, user-focused, and easy to navigate from the 
start.

