9. A user visits a website but leaves within 10 seconds because they cannot find the navigationmenu, which is hidden behind a small icon on a desktop screen. 
Analyze this design failureusing the ”KISS” (Keep It Simple) principle.
The KISS (Keep It Simple) principle emphasizes that designs should be simple, clear, and easy to understand. In this case, hiding the navigation menu behind a 
small icon on a desktop screen violates this principle.
Desktop users expect visible menus because there is enough screen space. When the navigation is hidden, users cannot immediately see where to go, leading to 
confusion. As a result, the user leaves the website within 10 seconds, which clearly indicates poor usability.
This design failure shows that simplicity was sacrificed for style. Instead of making navigation obvious, the designer made users search for it. According to 
the KISS principle, important elements like navigation should always be visible and easy to access, especially on large screens. A simple, visible menu would
have improved user experience and reduced bounce rate.

10. An e-commerce website has a URL structure like www.shop.com/prod?id=55&cat=9. Explain why this is bad for both users and Search Engines (SEO), and propose a 
betterstructure using Page Slugs.
The URL www.shop.com/prod?id=55&cat=9
 is bad for both users and search engines. For users, this URL is confusing and meaningless. It gives no idea about what the page contains, making it hard to 
trust, remember, or share.
From an SEO perspective, search engines prefer URLs that clearly describe the page content. Random parameters do not include keywords, so search engines 
struggle to understand and rank the page properly.
A better approach is to use page slugs, which are readable and descriptive.
Example of a better URL:
www.shop.com/electronics/wireless-headphones
This structure is user-friendly, improves SEO, and clearly communicates the product category and name. It enhances visibility in search results and creates a 
better overall experience.
